  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 PM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nA largely stagnant synoptic pattern called an Omega Block will allow \nfor mild and dry conditions to continue. Upper air observations\ndepict a large upper level ridge continuing to build over the \ncentral CONUS this morning; as this feature gradually continues \nto amplify in the coming days, it will shift eastward over the \nGreat Lakes. Sinking air underneath the upper level ridge will \nresult our prolonged stretch of dry conditions, mild \ntemperatures, and light winds continuing. High temperatures will\nbe in the upper 60s to low 70s and lows in the low to mid 40s \neach day. With the Omega Block expected to break down by the\nmiddle of next week, there are some signals in long range model\nguidance that a pattern change is ahead by the end of the month\ninto early November. Warmer than normal temperatures are likely\nto persist, however, a flip to more active, wetter weather is \npossible by late next week just in time for Halloween.\n\nWith the stagnant pattern locked in place, clear skies and light \nnortheasterly flow off of Lake Erie will likely yield some patchy \ndense advection fog overnight into early Saturday morning. Best \nchances for fog will likely be northeast of Fort Wayne, IN as this \nis where the smallest dewpoint depressions and best moisture will be \nfor fog to form. Much like the past few nights, the best timeframe \nfor dense fog will be between 3 AM to 10 AM EDT Saturday, with the \nlowest visibilities around daybreak. A SPS or Dense Fog Advisory may \nbe needed overnight depending on trends and where any fog\ndevelops.",
